course: biology
lesson: 179
title: Caves: bat clouds, blind fish, the olm, glowworms and a sealed cave
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Walk a little way into a cave and the daylight disappears; deeper in, it is pitch black all day and all year, so eyes stop being useful.
explain: Cave animals find their way in other ways: bats and swiftlets click and listen to the echoes, cave crickets feel the way with very long feelers, and some fish and the pale olm have lost their eyes altogether.
explain: Food is scarce in the dark, so a lot of it comes from outside: bats fly out at sunset to eat insects and come back to drop their droppings, which feed beetles and worms on the cave floor.
explain: In Romania there is even a cave sealed off for millions of years where the air smells of rotten eggs, and the whole food chain starts with bacteria eating chemicals instead of sunlight.
fact: Bracken Cave in Texas has millions of bats that fly out at dusk to eat insects, a cloud so big it shows on weather radar, and they eat tons of insects every night.
fact: Bats find their way by echolocation, making clicks and listening to the echoes, but bats are not blind: many see well, and fruit bats use eyes and noses more than echoes.
fact: Blind cave fish have lost their eyes because in total darkness eyes are useless and making them costs energy; Mexican cave fish have river relatives with eyes.
fact: The olm is a blind, pink cave salamander from Slovenia and Croatia that lives in water, can go years without food and may live about a hundred years.
fact: In Waitomo Caves in New Zealand, glowworms, which are fly larvae (young flies, like maggots), glow to lure insects into sticky threads and make the ceiling look like a starry sky.
fact: Cave swiftlets are among the very few birds that echolocate, and some swiftlets build nests from their own saliva.
fact: Cave crickets have very long legs and feelers because in the dark touch is more useful than sight.
fact: Bat droppings, called guano, pile into huge heaps that feed cave beetles and worms, and guano was once mined as valuable fertilizer.
fact: Cave animals are cut off from each other, so many caves have kinds found nowhere else, and tiny animals live two kilometres down in Krubera Cave in Georgia.
fact: Stalactites grow from dripping water that leaves behind a tiny bit of chalky mineral, over thousands of years, and touching them can stop them growing, because oil from skin stops the mineral sticking.
fact: Movile Cave in Romania was sealed off for millions of years until 1986; bacteria make food from chemicals, and dozens of kinds of animals live there, most of them nowhere else.
exercise: Why do the bats of Bracken Cave fly out at dusk? | To eat insects | To find water only | To sleep in the trees | To escape the rain
exercise: How big is the bat cloud from Bracken Cave? | Big enough to show on weather radar | About ten bats flying together | Too small to see | As big as one tree in the park outside
exercise: How do bats fly through a dark cave without hitting the walls? | They click and listen to the echoes | They glow in the dark like little lamps | They follow the walls with their wingtips | They fly very slowly, feeling their way by touch
exercise: Are bats blind? | No, many see well, and fruit bats use eyes and noses a lot | Yes, all bats are completely blind | Yes, bats have no eyes | Only baby bats can see
exercise: Why have blind cave fish lost their eyes? | Eyes are useless in the dark and cost energy to make | Bats ate their eyes | They closed them for too long, many years ago, and forgot how to open them | The water melted them
exercise: Where does the olm live? | In water in deep caves in Slovenia and Croatia | In the treetops of the great rainforests of Brazil | In deserts in Africa | On icebergs that float in the cold Arctic sea
exercise: What is remarkable about the olm? | It can go years without food and may live about a hundred years | It can fly short distances | It glows bright green | It lives only one week, so it must eat fast and lay eggs quickly
exercise: What makes the blue lights on the ceiling of Waitomo Caves? | Glowworms, fly larvae that lure insects | Tiny electric lamps put up by visitors | Glowing rocks | Stars shining through holes in the roof
exercise: Why do the glowworms glow? | To attract insects into their sticky threads | To scare away the bats that hunt them at night | To keep warm | To light the way for tourists
exercise: How do cave swiftlets find their way in the dark? | They click and use the echoes | They follow the bats out of the cave | They smell their way along the walls | They wait for daylight to come in
exercise: Why do cave crickets have such long legs and feelers? | In the dark, touch is more useful than sight | To jump right out of the cave | To catch the bats that fly past them in the dark | To look scary
exercise: What is guano? | Bat droppings that pile up in caves | A blind cave fish | A kind of long, pointed stalactite on the roof | A bat's wing
exercise: Why was guano once valuable? | It was mined as fertilizer | It was used as money in markets | People cooked and ate it in hard times | It was used to make glass for windows
exercise: Why do many caves have animals found nowhere else? | Cave animals are cut off from each other | Cave animals fly from cave to cave every night | Scientists put them there | All caves share the same animals
exercise: How do stalactites form? | Dripping water leaves tiny bits of chalky mineral over thousands of years | Icicles turn to stone overnight | Bats build them | They grow like plants
exercise: Why should visitors not touch stalactites? | Skin oil can stop them growing | They are red hot | They bite | They break into dust at once if anyone touches them
exercise: How do animals in sealed Movile Cave get food without sunlight? | Bacteria make food from chemicals, and other animals eat them | Visitors feed them every day through a hole | Sunlight leaks in through cracks | They eat the rocks directly with their strong teeth
test: «blind as a bat lol» What is right? | Bats are not blind: many see well and also use echoes | Right, bats have no eyes | Right, bats see only at noon | Bats see only colours, not shapes, so they bump into every wall and tree
test: In Batman films a huge swarm of bats bursts out of a cave. Where do real millions of bats do that every evening? | Bracken Cave in Texas | On the Moon | In Antarctica, under the ice | Under the sea
test: In cartoons, Dracula the vampire bat drinks blood in a castle. What do most bats from Bracken Cave really eat? | Insects | Blood | Garlic | Bread and cheese
test: «saw 4 cave crickets, 6 legs n 2 feelers each, how many legs» | 24 | 32 | 8 | 16
test: «the olm's got 3 toes on each front foot n 2 on each back foot, how many toes on one olm» | 10 | 5 | 12 | 20
test: What would happen to eyes in a fish family that lives in a dark cave for many, many generations? | Eyes could shrink and disappear, since they are useless there | The fish would grow bigger eyes to see better | The fish would grow wings | Nothing ever changes in any animal, not even in a million years
test: «the cave in Romania stinks of rotten eggs, nothing can live there» What is right? | Dozens of kinds live there, many found nowhere else | Right, nothing lives there | Only bats live there, and only in the summer months | It is full of rabbits
test: «my bro touched a stalactite, so what, it's a rock» What is right? | Skin oil can stop it growing, and it took thousands of years to form | It will grow faster | It grows back in a day | Touching makes it glow
test: What would happen to the beetles on a cave floor if all the bats left? | They would lose much of their food, the guano | They would get more food | They would turn into bats | Nothing, because they eat sunlight and need no food from bats
test: «glowworms r worms that glow» What is right? | They are fly larvae, not true worms | Right, they are earthworms that glow | They are tiny fish that glow in the dark | They are glowing stones that look like worms
test: Which bird could find its way in a pitch-black cave? | A cave swiftlet, by clicking | A pigeon, by its sense of smell | A chicken, by its sharp eyesight | An ostrich, by running very fast
exam: Identify the means by which bats locate obstacles and prey in complete darkness. | They emit clicks and interpret the returning echoes | They perceive heat with their wings | They rely exclusively upon touch | They emit light from their mouths
exam: Ascertain the reason for which certain cave fish have lost their eyes. | Eyes confer no benefit in total darkness and their maintenance consumes energy | The eyes were removed by predators | The cave water dissolves eyes | They are born without eyes for one generation only
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the glowworms of the Waitomo Caves? | They are fly larvae which emit light to lure insects into sticky threads | They are luminous minerals within the rock | They are earthworms that glow when touched | They are lamps installed for the benefit of visitors
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning Movile Cave. | It was sealed for millions of years, and its food chain begins with bacteria consuming chemicals | It is illuminated by sunlight through its roof | It contains no living organisms | It was discovered in the year 1086
